In the regime of [[def-nondegenerate-root-of-unity-level-one-hecke-regime]] (both branches (C0) and (CP)), fixing an admissible normalising series, the blockwise graded isomorphism $\rho_\alpha:R^\Lambda_\alpha\to H^\Lambda_\alpha$ of [[thm-brundan-kleshchev-blockwise-hecke-klr-isomorphism]] with the grading of [[cor-cyclotomic-hecke-blocks-inherit-a-nontrivial-z-grading]] transports the standard homogeneous cyclotomic data to the Hecke side, block by block and then by the restricted direct sums of [[def-graded-module-and-projective-grothendieck-groups-for-klr-towers]]:

1. the unique $A$-module isomorphism $\phi:K_0^{\rm gr}(R^\Lambda)\to V_A(\Lambda_0)$ of [[thm-cyclotomic-klr-projective-k-zero-is-the-integral-highest-weight-module]] (sending the vacuum class to $v_{\Lambda_0}$ and intertwining the normalized divided powers $E_i,F_i,K_i$) becomes an $A$-module isomorphism $\phi^{\rm Hecke}:K_0^{\rm gr}(H^{\Lambda})\to V_A(\Lambda_0)$ with the same properties;
2. the canonical identification $\Psi_{\rm can}:G_0^{\rm gr}(R^\Lambda)\to V_A(\Lambda_0)^\vee$ of [[thm-cyclotomic-klr-module-g-zero-is-the-dual-integral-highest-weight-module]] (restricted weight dual, canonical module action, normalized simple classes to the form-dual projective basis) becomes an isomorphism $\Psi_{\rm can}^{\rm Hecke}:G_0^{\rm gr}(H^{\Lambda})\to V_A(\Lambda_0)^\vee$ with the same properties;
3. the two identifications intertwine the transported $E_i,F_i,K_i$ of [[thm-cyclotomic-klr-functors-define-an-integrable-quantum-action-on-k-zero-and-g-zero]] with the Hecke-side functors induced by [[def-cyclotomic-klr-induction-and-restriction-functors]], and the pairing of [[def-graded-cartan-pairing-between-projectives-and-modules]] with the tensor/Hom pairing of loc. cit., which is weightwise perfect in the normalized projective/simple bases;
4. in branch (C0), and with the additional hypotheses of [[thm-self-dual-indecomposable-cyclotomic-klr-projectives-give-the-lower-global-basis]] and [[thm-self-dual-simple-cyclotomic-klr-modules-give-the-upper-global-basis]] (symmetric Cartan datum with $d_i=1$, characteristic zero, finite loopless quiver with arbitrary edge multiplicities and $Q_{ij}=(-1)^{h_{ij}}(u-v)^{h_{ij}+h_{ji}}$), which the type-$A$ specialization of the regime satisfies, the transported normalized $\#$self-dual indecomposable projectives correspond bijectively to the lower global basis and the transported normalized $D_\psi$-self-dual simples to the upper global basis; the transported simple and projective crystals are the $B(\Lambda_0)$-crystals of [[thm-simple-and-indecomposable-projective-crystals-agree-with-b-lambda]], so each class carries its crystal label in $RP$.

No based-quotient, quantum-star or cyclotomic categorification statement is asserted beyond these transported clauses. Choice is inherited only through the cited global-basis suppliers.

In the situation of [[def-graded-specht-module-by-homogeneous-standard-tableaux]], define d^A(lambda) for an ADDABLE i-node A to be the number of addable i-nodes strictly ABOVE A minus the number of removable i-nodes strictly ABOVE A. Define the codegree of a standard tableau $T$ by $\operatorname{codeg}(\varnothing):=0$ and $\operatorname{codeg}(T):=d^A(\mu^A)+\operatorname{codeg}(T_{\le d-1})$, where $A$ is the node containing $d$ and $\mu^A=\mu\setminus A$. Define the defect of a residue content $\alpha\in Q_+$ by $\operatorname{def}(\alpha):=(\Lambda_0,\alpha)-(\alpha,\alpha)/2$ (the symmetric form of [[def-cyclic-affine-type-a-cartan-datum]]). Then for every partition $\mu$ with content $\alpha$ and every standard $\mu$-tableau $T$:

1. $d_A(\mu)+d^A(\mu^A)=d_i(\mu)+1$ where $i$ is the residue of the removable node $A$ and $d_i(\mu)=\#\{\text{addable }i\text{-nodes of }\mu\}-\#\{\text{removable }i\text{-nodes of }\mu\}$;
2. $d_i(\mu)=(\Lambda_0-\alpha,\alpha_i)$;
3. $\operatorname{def}(\alpha)=\operatorname{def}(\alpha-\alpha_i)+d_i(\mu)+1$;
4. $\deg(T)+\operatorname{codeg}(T)=\operatorname{def}(\alpha)$;
5. removing a brick does not change the codegree: if $m\to_T m+1\to_T\cdots\to_T d$ and $S=T_{\le m-1}$, then $\operatorname{codeg}(S)=\operatorname{codeg}(T)$ whenever (i) $m,\dots,d$ occupy nodes in the first row of the first component of $T$, or (ii) $d-m+1=e$ (the quantum characteristic).

No choice is used.

In branch (C0) of the regime of [[def-nondegenerate-root-of-unity-level-one-hecke-regime]], with the normalized self-dual simples $D(\lambda)$ and their normalized projective covers $Y(\lambda)$ labelled by the crystal $B(\Lambda_0)=RP$ as in [[prop-qg8-categorification-transports-across-the-hecke-klr-bridge]] (4):

1. the transported isomorphism $\phi^{\rm Hecke}:K_0^{\rm gr}(H^{\Lambda})\to V_A(\Lambda_0)$ sends the class of the normalized self-dual indecomposable projective $Y(\lambda)$ to the lower global basis vector $G(\lambda)$ for every $\lambda\in RP$;
2. the transported identification $\Psi_{\rm can}^{\rm Hecke}:G_0^{\rm gr}(H^{\Lambda})\to V_A(\Lambda_0)^\vee$ sends $[D(\lambda)]$ to the upper global basis vector $G^*(\lambda)$, form-dual to $G(\lambda)$ under the highest-weight contravariant form;
3. equivalently the lower global basis is the canonical basis of the basic module $V_A(\Lambda_0)$ and corresponds to projective indecomposables, while the upper global basis is the dual-canonical basis and corresponds to simple classes, and the labels are those of [[prop-qg8-categorification-transports-across-the-hecke-klr-bridge]] (4): the crystal $B(\Lambda_0)=RP$, with a simple and its projective cover carrying the same label. The passage from these crystal labels to the cellular/Dipper-James parametrization of the simple modules is not asserted here; it is established with the head classification later on this page;
4. specializing the grading $q=1$ via the forgetful maps $\zeta$ of [[def-graded-module-and-projective-grothendieck-groups-for-klr-towers]] recovers Ariki's ungraded canonical-basis statement, and combined with the graded decomposition theorem later on this page it gives his ungraded decomposition-number theorem.

Choice is inherited only through the QG-8 global-basis suppliers; no choice is introduced by the transport or by the label comparison.

In the regime of [[def-nondegenerate-root-of-unity-level-one-hecke-regime]], for $\lambda\in RP_\alpha$ the graded Specht module $S(\lambda)$ of [[def-graded-specht-module-by-homogeneous-standard-tableaux]] has irreducible head, denoted $\dot D(\lambda)$, and $\dot D(\lambda)^\circledast\cong\dot D(\lambda)$. Moreover:

1. the modules $\{\dot D(\lambda)\langle m\rangle:\lambda\in RP_\alpha,\ m\in\mathbb Z\}$ are a complete set of pairwise non-isomorphic graded simple $R^\Lambda_\alpha$-modules (equivalently, of $H^\Lambda_\alpha$ with the transported grading);
2. in $G_0^{\rm gr}(H^\Lambda_\alpha)$ one has $[S(\lambda)]=[\dot D(\lambda)]+(\ast)$ if $\lambda$ is restricted and $[S(\lambda)]=(\ast)$ otherwise, where $(\ast)$ is an $A$-linear combination of $[\dot D(\mu)]$'s with $\mu\lhd\lambda$; consequently $[S(\mu):\dot D(\lambda)]_q\ne0$ implies $\lambda\unlhd\mu$, and $[S(\lambda):\dot D(\lambda)]_q=1$;
3. in branch (C0), $\dot D(\lambda)\cong D(\lambda)$, the normalized simple with crystal label $\lambda$ of [[prop-qg8-categorification-transports-across-the-hecke-klr-bridge]] (4); equivalently, in the Dipper-James convention recorded in [[def-type-a-hecke-specht-module-and-cell-form]] the simple $\dot D(\lambda)$ carries the $e$-regular label $\lambda'$, the conjugate of the restricted partition $\lambda$.

Parts (1) and (2) hold in both branches (C0) and (CP); part (3) is stated in branch (C0), the branch of the decomposition theorem it consumes. The branch (CP) analogue of (3) is the identification [BK 2009, Theorem 5.18] and is NOT asserted here. Choice is inherited only through the cited transported global-basis suppliers, as recorded in the contract.

